WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.060 --> 00:00:04.020
This is hemoglobin, the oxygen
carrier in your red blood cells.

2
00:00:04.060 --> 00:00:07.220
Four subunits: two alpha, two beta.

3
00:00:07.260 --> 00:00:11.220
Each one holds a heme,
and each heme holds one iron.

4
00:00:11.260 --> 00:00:13.620
Four irons, four oxygens.

5
00:00:13.660 --> 00:00:17.380
The interesting part is not
that it binds oxygen.

6
00:00:17.420 --> 00:00:20.780
It is that the four
sites talk to each other.

7
00:00:23.367 --> 00:00:25.887
Let's go into one beta subunit.

8
00:00:25.927 --> 00:00:28.207
Here is the heme, seen edge-on.

9
00:00:28.247 --> 00:00:32.177
The iron sits in the middle,
but notice: it is not in the plane.

10
00:00:32.217 --> 00:00:33.697
It is pulled slightly toward this

11
00:00:33.737 --> 00:00:37.567
histidine below it,
the proximal histidine.

12
00:00:37.607 --> 00:00:40.127
The iron has six coordination sites:

13
00:00:40.167 --> 00:00:42.927
four go to the pyrrole
nitrogens of the ring,

14
00:00:42.967 --> 00:00:47.727
and the fifth is a coordination bond
from the histidine's imidazole nitrogen.

15
00:00:47.767 --> 00:00:51.407
That bond is what anchors
the heme to the protein.

16
00:00:51.447 --> 00:00:56.177
Above the iron is the distal
histidine in an empty pocket.

17
00:00:56.217 --> 00:00:58.657
This is the T state, the tense state.

18
00:00:58.697 --> 00:01:00.317
Low affinity.

19
00:01:01.607 --> 00:01:03.087
Now oxygen arrives.

20
00:01:03.127 --> 00:01:08.127
It binds on the distal side, and the iron
moves into the plane of the heme.

21
00:01:08.167 --> 00:01:11.727
We have exaggerated the motion
three times so you can see it;

22
00:01:11.767 --> 00:01:15.427
the real shift is less
than half an angstrom.

23
00:01:15.467 --> 00:01:19.187
Small, but the proximal histidine
is attached to the iron,

24
00:01:19.227 --> 00:01:24.127
so it moves too, and it drags
its helix, helix F, with it.

25
00:01:24.167 --> 00:01:28.987
A chemical event at one atom has become
a mechanical event in the protein.

26
00:01:29.747 --> 00:01:30.747
Pull back.

27
00:01:30.787 --> 00:01:35.097
The helix shift is transmitted
to the interface between the subunits.

28
00:01:35.667 --> 00:01:38.567
Watch the ghost:
that is where the T state was.

29
00:01:38.607 --> 00:01:41.527
The alpha-two beta-two
pair rotates about fifteen

30
00:01:41.567 --> 00:01:44.967
degrees relative to alpha-one beta-one.

31
00:01:45.007 --> 00:01:47.577
This is a T-to-R transition.

32
00:01:47.617 --> 00:01:49.657
Along the interface there are ion pairs,

33
00:01:49.697 --> 00:01:53.417
salt bridges, that hold
the T state together.

34
00:01:53.457 --> 00:01:56.687
The last residue of each
chain does most of the work:

35
00:01:56.727 --> 00:01:59.567
the C-terminal histidine
of beta and the C-terminal

36
00:01:59.607 --> 00:02:03.637
arginine of alpha each make two of them.

37
00:02:03.677 --> 00:02:06.357
As the subunits rotate, they break.

38
00:02:06.397 --> 00:02:10.757
Once they are broken, the remaining
empty hemes are in the R state,

39
00:02:10.797 --> 00:02:13.737
the relaxed state, with high affinity.

40
00:02:13.777 --> 00:02:19.427
That is cooperativity: the first
oxygen makes the next three easier.

41
00:02:20.627 --> 00:02:23.147
Look down the axis
between the beta chains.

42
00:02:23.187 --> 00:02:28.117
In the T state there is a cavity
here lined with positive charges.

43
00:02:28.157 --> 00:02:33.317
This is where 2,3-bisphosphoglycerate
binds and holds the T state.

44
00:02:33.357 --> 00:02:36.867
In R the cavity narrows
and BPG is squeezed out.

45
00:02:36.907 --> 00:02:40.377
We'll come back to BPG in another video.

46
00:02:41.627 --> 00:02:44.547
One more thing lives at this interface.

47
00:02:44.587 --> 00:02:51.427
Histidine 146, the last residue of the
beta chain, sits next to aspartate 94.

48
00:02:51.467 --> 00:02:57.107
In the T state that aspartate raises
the histidine's pKa, so it holds a proton.

49
00:02:57.147 --> 00:03:00.307
When the protein goes to R,
the pair separates,

50
00:03:00.347 --> 00:03:03.377
the pKa drops, and the proton leaves.

51
00:03:03.417 --> 00:03:05.057
Now run it backwards.

52
00:03:05.097 --> 00:03:07.777
In the working muscle,
where protons are abundant,

53
00:03:07.817 --> 00:03:11.057
the proton goes on, the ion pair re-forms,

54
00:03:11.097 --> 00:03:13.657
T is favored, and oxygen is released.

55
00:03:13.697 --> 00:03:17.627
That is the Bohr effect,
and this is where it lives.

56
00:03:18.827 --> 00:03:23.507
Here is the same story as a curve:
oxygen saturation against oxygen pressure.

57
00:03:23.547 --> 00:03:27.447
T is the low-affinity end,
R is the high-affinity end,

58
00:03:27.487 --> 00:03:31.117
and the sigmoid shape between them
is the switch we just watched.

59
00:03:31.157 --> 00:03:34.397
Protons and BPG push
the curve to the right.

60
00:03:34.437 --> 00:03:37.997
In the next video, we'll look
at an enzyme that does chemistry,

61
00:03:38.037 --> 00:03:40.507
not just binding: chymotrypsin.

